# tools/al/ales_stereo_split_silence_witness.rish -- stereo_split_silence: the auto-splitter carried into stereo, one cut line for both channels, the stereo twin of the silence stripper (ALES149).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES149.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-052853_fill-ales149-lotus-stereo-split-silence.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147 -- the stereo voice-activity segmenter, run once to read BOTH channels' silences over one shared frame grid, its segment_stereo, its StereoSegments, its StereoSegmentError, reused
# whole), lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 -- the mono segmenter and max_segments, through ALES147), lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye (ALES148 -- the stereo silence stripper, the join-law twin, imported by the selftest), lotus/split_silence.rye
# (ALES97 -- the mono auto-splitter, the shape carried up), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 -- the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, its splice, its max_clip, the EditError reused
# whole), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 -- the .silent verdict). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, so their
# silences differ in count and boundary, and splitting each channel by its own silence would cut the two speakers at different positions and tear every take apart.
#
# lotus/stereo_split_silence.rye decides ONE cut line for both channels -- the silence intersection: segment both channels over one shared frame grid, decide a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both channels, and split
# only at jointly-silent frames. A stereo take is a maximal span of frames that is NOT jointly-silent -- a region either speaker fills -- cut identically from both channels, so every take is a balanced StereoClip; a frame silent in only
# one channel keeps the open take running, because a sound panned hard left is real audio the right's silence must never split away. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once through ALES147's own up-front checks
# before any take is written (BadThreshold on an illegal band, BadRange on a span past the samples, BadFloor on a floor above the peak ceiling, BadFrame on a zero/oversized/non-dividing length), so a refusal leaves out empty and both
# channels untouched; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole. The crux is that it invents no new measurement and no new sample -- it reads its silences through ALES147 and copies whole samples through ALES2's splice -- so it can
# never drift from the segmenter it composes; what is new is the composition rule, the intersection of two independent silences read as one cut line, whose whole honesty is that re-joining the splitter's pieces returns exactly ALES148's trimmed master.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_split_silence_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-split-silence: Language EN — stereo_split_silence splits a stereo master on its JOINT silence, writing one take per maximal not-jointly-silent region. It segments both channels of the whole master into frames of one length through ALES147's stereo segmenter, decides a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both channels, then coalesces every span of consecutive not-jointly-silent frames into one shared take — a start-and-count span serving both channels, so every take is a balanced StereoClip. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; a frame silent in only one channel keeps the open take running, because the other speaker still carries sound, and only a moment where neither speaker carries sound is a shared pause that ends a take. The split is read-only — it names shared spans and mutates not one sample of the source; stereo_take_clip copies whole not-jointly-silent samples on both channels through ALES2's splice into a fresh balanced destination. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once before any take so a refusal leaves out empty and both channels untouched."
say "ales-stereo-split-silence: Style Radiant — ALES97 splits a mono clip at its silence, keeping each non-silent region as its own take, the auto-splitter every recorder, sampler, and podcast tool ships. Carried into stereo it meets the wall ALES148 met: ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, their silences differing in count and boundary, so splitting each channel by its own silence would cut the two speakers at different positions and tear every take apart. This rung decides one cut line that serves both — the silence intersection: split only where a frame is silent in both channels, keep every frame either speaker fills. A stereo take is a maximal span of frames that is not jointly-silent, cut identically from both channels, so every take stays balanced. The crux is that it invents no new measurement and no new sample — it reads its silences through ALES147, which reads them through ALES95, ALES94, and the two readers those fuse, and copies whole samples through ALES2's splice, so it can never drift from the segmenter it composes. What is new is the composition rule, the intersection of two independent silences read as one cut line, and its whole honesty is that re-joining the splitter's pieces returns exactly ALES148's trimmed master. The crux stands out as the stereo take law (the index-th take clip equals the original over its shared span on both channels and out.len equals the not-jointly-silent-region count, across many frame lengths, floors, and splits, every take balanced), the join law (concatenating every take clip in order per channel equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte), the preservation law (a frame quiet on the left yet loud on the right kept in its take, no false split), and the degenerate and atomicity laws (an all-jointly-silent master yielding zero takes, a master loud on at least one channel per frame yielding one whole-master take, the source byte-for-byte unchanged, an empty clip and a ragged frame length and an inverted band and an illegal floor each refusing by name with out left empty)."
say "ales-stereo-split-silence: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) read through one shared frame length, band, floor, and split; the balance invariant is asserted on entry, and all validation runs once up front through ALES147's segment_stereo (ALES92's precheck for band and span, the floor against meter's sample_peak_max, the frame-length divisibility) read-only before a single take is named, so a refusal leaves out empty and both channels untouched; the forward pass advances one monotonic cursor per channel across its own tiling run list, decides each frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both, and coalesces consecutive not-jointly-silent frames into one shared StereoTake (a start-and-count span into both channels, sized by max_stereo_takes = the segmenter's own run ceiling), asserting each cursor names the run covering the frame start and that the extension stays contiguous; stereo_take_clip materializes the index-th take into a fresh balanced StereoClip by copying both channels' samples over the shared span through ALES2's splice, asserting the destination begins empty and leaves balanced; the stereo take law is proven against an independent by-hand reference (a second implementation reading frame verdicts by run scan and coalescing by hand) across eight frame lengths, four floors, and three splits, the join law proves the takes re-joined per channel equal ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte, the preservation law proves a left-silent right-loud frame keeps its take, and the degenerate and atomicity cases leave out empty; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole plus a named-honest TakesFull, siloed to lotus/, the split reading only, copying whole samples and fabricating none, no adaptive floor, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_split_silence"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_split_silence.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-split-silence: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_split_silence.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-split-silence: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-split-silence: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-split-silence' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-split-silence: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-split-silence line"
